		<link href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/viewtopic.php?p=846#p846"/>
		<title type="html"><![CDATA[LCP solvers and nonpenetration CCD]]></title>

		
		<content type="html" xml:base="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/viewtopic.php?p=846#p846"><![CDATA[
I'm now recommending a sequential impulse algorithm over the PGS algorithm. While these two algorithms give similar results, it is conceptually simpler to implement sequential impulses.<br><br>Download slides and code here:<br><a href="http://www.gphysics.com/gdc-2006/" class="postlink">http://www.gphysics.com/gdc-2006/</a><br><br>Within the slides, I show how to include weight feeling friction. Also, deltaT is not needed for the impulse calculations.<br><br>Caveat: If you don't plan to allow penetration, you must at least have a slop region. Otherwise you will end up cutting your time-step extremely small. My opinion is that CCD should be used to prevent tunneling, but should not be used for objects that are already touching.<br><br>You can achieve restitution by simply augmenting the bias velocity (mentioned in the slides) with a restitution term.<br><br>Now if you really want an accurate simulation you will have to implement an O(n^3)+ LCP solver (see Mihai Anitescu's work). If you can use the normal force from the previous step for friction, you might consider using a conjugate gradient algorithm like GPCG, which is O(n)+.<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=12">Erin Catto</a> — Sun Apr 02, 2006 11:14 pm</p><hr />
]]></content>
